1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:08,380
A revolutionary symbol of industrial superiority, a production technique that transformed nations and transfixed the world.

2
00:00:08,960 --> 00:00:14,080
Now, the unrelenting base and prolific production of Assembly Lines on Modern Marvels.

3
00:00:34,311 --> 00:00:39,840
I think the assembly line is the most significant technological development of the 20th century.

4
00:00:39,841 --> 00:00:44,080
everything that we use, we would not have it in the quantities

5
00:00:44,180 --> 00:00:47,780
we have it and at the prices we have it

6
00:00:47,880 --> 00:00:50,180
without the assembly line.

7
00:00:50,280 --> 00:00:54,580
The assembly line moves the world.

8
00:00:54,680 --> 00:00:58,200
It is the driving force behind every industrial nation on earth,

9
00:00:58,300 --> 00:01:01,600
workers and machines organized in a continuous flow of raw

10
00:01:01,700 --> 00:01:05,740
materials made into products on an unimaginable scale.

11
00:01:05,840 --> 00:01:07,840
I think the best way to define an assembly line is the way

12
00:01:07,841 --> 00:01:11,240
the way Ford did. He called it progressive production.

13
00:01:11,241 --> 00:01:15,960
Work moves to the person and moves past the person rather than the person going to the

14
00:01:15,961 --> 00:01:20,180
work. Parts are added until you come up with a final product.

15
00:01:20,181 --> 00:01:25,760
And that's what the assembly line is, essentially a work in motion principle.

16
00:01:25,761 --> 00:01:33,936
Work in motion is as mesmerizing today as it was at the turn of the 20th century.

17
00:01:33,937 --> 00:01:34,637
What

18
00:01:34,638 --> 00:01:39,160
may look like miles of mechanized mayhem, is actually one of the most dependable and

19
00:01:39,161 --> 00:01:41,340
durable production techniques ever invented.

20
00:01:42,620 --> 00:01:49,340
Today, the assembly line is used to make everything from a two-pound computer to a 94,000-pound

21
00:01:49,341 --> 00:01:50,041
aircraft.

22
00:01:52,580 --> 00:01:59,580
The Boeing 717-200 will soon be the largest vehicle ever built on a moving assembly line.

23
00:01:59,581 --> 00:02:08,300
300 yards long, the line will move the 100-seat aircraft one-half inch every minute and deliver

24
00:02:08,301 --> 00:02:12,820
to customers at the rate of one every four days.

25
00:02:12,821 --> 00:02:18,340
Boeing's 21st Century assembly line reflects the same audacity of scale found in the technique's

26
00:02:18,341 --> 00:02:23,280
origins.

27
00:02:23,281 --> 00:02:27,680
the very beginning, the assembly line was first and foremost about the need for speed.

28
00:02:29,520 --> 00:02:33,760
Industrial society was highly advanced at the time the assembly line came into being,

29
00:02:34,560 --> 00:02:42,960
but once it came into being, it was so productive that very few things were made without the line

30
00:02:42,961 --> 00:02:43,661
after that.

31
00:02:43,662 --> 00:02:47,760
There are several factors that went into the making of the assembly line.

32
00:02:47,761 --> 00:02:56,400
The first had to be that the parts of any product that would be made on an assembly line had to be interchangeable.

33
00:02:58,480 --> 00:03:06,180
Interchangeable or uniform parts were first invented in 1813 by Connecticut gunmaker Simeon North for his pistol

34
00:03:06,181 --> 00:03:09,360
and later refined in the manufacture of firearms.

35
00:03:11,040 --> 00:03:17,140
Uniform parts and the simplicity of attaching them to each other was a pivotal innovation in the history of mass assembly.

36
00:03:17,760 --> 00:03:27,760
The primary inspiration for putting work in motion originated in the 19th century disassembly lines of Cincinnati meatpacking facilities.

37
00:03:27,761 --> 00:03:41,761
The idea being that a cow or a pig was grabbed up by the hind legs and essentially put on a moving line and was successively slaughtered, gutted, and then butchered

38
00:03:43,353 --> 00:03:45,920
all in a moving line basis.

39
00:03:45,921 --> 00:03:56,600
In the 1850s, simple conveyors developed by textile manufacturers were used to move heavy molds filled with hot iron in the production of steel.

40
00:03:58,040 --> 00:04:08,800
Several decades later, can manufacturers created an elaborate system of conveyors designed to move specific work to specific workers on an assembly line basis.

41
00:04:08,801 --> 00:04:14,480
And there in can making you have various machines that are

42
00:04:14,481 --> 00:04:18,240
linked together by conveyors, which cans are built, taken from

43
00:04:18,241 --> 00:04:22,700
sheet steel and built up into full cans.

44
00:04:25,340 --> 00:04:30,920
Henry Ford, who built his first car in 1896, was unique among

45
00:04:30,921 --> 00:04:34,160
automobile inventors, not only because he sought to build a car

46
00:04:34,161 --> 00:04:37,320
for the masses, but because he wanted to build one that was the

47
00:04:37,321 --> 00:04:40,760
lowest priced. It wasn't until he developed the wildly

48
00:04:40,761 --> 00:04:44,480
successful Model T that Ford began experimenting with available

49
00:04:44,481 --> 00:04:50,080
production techniques. By 1908, the nation was on the move,

50
00:04:50,081 --> 00:04:53,320
and Ford's need for production speed was paramount.

51
00:04:53,321 --> 00:04:56,520
You have to understand the context in which the assembly

52
00:04:56,521 --> 00:05:02,480
line took place at Ford. From the time that the Model T

53
00:05:02,481 --> 00:05:06,680
was introduced, the Ford Motor Company could not meet the demand

54
00:05:06,681 --> 00:05:08,980
for Model T's.

55
00:05:09,300 --> 00:05:12,360
And his goal was never to create the assembly line.

56
00:05:12,361 --> 00:05:16,560
His goal was to constantly improve production

57
00:05:16,561 --> 00:05:20,080
to drive the price of his car down,

58
00:05:20,980 --> 00:05:23,300
but also keep the quality up

59
00:05:23,301 --> 00:05:25,740
and keep the production volume going up.

60
00:05:26,180 --> 00:05:29,120
And he pursued this goal relentlessly.

61
00:05:30,520 --> 00:05:33,440
Ford regularly rearranged men and machines

62
00:05:33,441 --> 00:05:36,620
to improve the speed of stationary component assembly.

63
00:05:36,680 --> 00:05:46,580
In 1910, Ford opened the 60-acre Highland Park plant and set up the machinery on the shop floor in the actual sequence of automobile production.

64
00:05:47,040 --> 00:05:55,880
This radical departure from the more conventional grouping of machines by type enabled workers to deliver parts more efficiently to each sub-assembly station.

65
00:05:56,880 --> 00:06:03,300
He was averaging a doubling of production for several years before the assembly line.

66
00:06:03,301 --> 00:06:15,200
But this relentless drive for ever more production at ever lower cost led him almost inexorably to the techniques that eventually developed into the assembly line.

67
00:06:15,480 --> 00:06:22,420
Ford then made the giant step of moving the car instead of the worker, based on the principles of Frederick Speedy Taylor.

68
00:06:23,100 --> 00:06:28,480
As the first authority in industrial efficiency, Taylor's work was already popular in the steel industry.

69
00:06:28,481 --> 00:06:34,280
It was his specific understanding that human intelligence, as

70
00:06:34,281 --> 00:06:38,960
applied by workers at the point of production, was a hindrance to efficient operation.

71
00:06:38,961 --> 00:06:44,420
The principles were designed to break down a particular job into smaller and smaller

72
00:06:44,421 --> 00:06:49,800
tasks, which meant that much of the task could be controlled, mechanized, and thus speeded

73
00:06:49,801 --> 00:06:54,420
up. This process would require more men with less skills.

74
00:06:54,421 --> 00:06:58,420
Effectively, he tailorized the work and took the skill out of

75
00:06:58,520 --> 00:07:02,620
the work process, dividing the labor, subdividing it again,

76
00:07:02,720 --> 00:07:05,960
so that a workman would do a smaller and smaller part of it.

77
00:07:06,060 --> 00:07:09,460
And doing the smaller and smaller piece of work,

78
00:07:09,560 --> 00:07:13,280
he could do it faster and faster.

79
00:07:13,380 --> 00:07:18,280
In April of 1913, 29 highly skilled Ford employees

80
00:07:18,380 --> 00:07:20,680
stood next to each other alongside a component

81
00:07:20,780 --> 00:07:22,420
sub-assembly line.

82
00:07:22,421 --> 00:07:25,420
The line was composed of all the parts necessary to make a

83
00:07:25,421 --> 00:07:28,420
flywheel magneto. Rather than assemble the entire

84
00:07:28,421 --> 00:07:32,420
component by himself, each man performed a simple, narrowly

85
00:07:32,421 --> 00:07:35,420
defined task on the component, and then passed the part on to

86
00:07:35,421 --> 00:07:38,420
the next guy. Then they said that if this

87
00:07:38,421 --> 00:07:41,420
works with transmissions or it works with magnetos, what about

88
00:07:41,421 --> 00:07:43,420
engines? What about front axles?

89
00:07:43,421 --> 00:07:47,420
What about rear axles? And eventually, they got all these

90
00:07:47,421 --> 00:07:51,420
sub-assemblies on the assembly line. One year later, after

91
00:07:51,421 --> 00:07:54,420
after Ford had successfully assembled key car components

92
00:07:54,421 --> 00:07:58,420
on an assembly line basis, a Model T chassis was pulled by

93
00:07:58,421 --> 00:08:02,420
rope across the factory floor. Workers began picking up parts

94
00:08:02,421 --> 00:08:05,420
from carefully placed piles of sub-assembly parts and attaching

95
00:08:05,421 --> 00:08:09,420
them. When they were finished, they had assembled an entire car

96
00:08:09,421 --> 00:08:13,420
in only 5 hours and 50 minutes. The moving assembly line had

97
00:08:13,421 --> 00:08:16,420
cut the production time of the Model T in half.

98
00:08:16,421 --> 00:08:28,160
Ford and Ford's engineers took all these ideas and brought them all together and created what we think of as the modern assembly line.

99
00:08:28,161 --> 00:08:32,200
But it was really an amalgam of all sorts of other ideas.

100
00:08:32,540 --> 00:08:39,360
But nobody had ever tried to put them all together and use them to put together something as complicated as an automobile.

101
00:08:39,361 --> 00:08:47,360
Fashioned after the conveyor belts used in slaughterhouses,

102
00:08:47,361 --> 00:08:50,360
Ford installed the first automatic conveyor belt to move

103
00:08:50,361 --> 00:08:53,360
the chassis down the line in a continuous flow.

104
00:08:53,361 --> 00:08:58,360
What used to take a man 728 hours to build could now be

105
00:08:58,361 --> 00:09:01,360
built on a moving assembly line in 90 minutes.

106
00:09:01,361 --> 00:09:07,360
Production soared dramatically, from 82,000 cars in 1912 to a

107
00:09:07,361 --> 00:09:12,160
to a staggering 267,000 cars in 1914.

108
00:09:12,161 --> 00:09:15,500
With that, Ford dropped the price of the average Model T

109
00:09:15,501 --> 00:09:19,160
from $600 to $490.

110
00:09:19,161 --> 00:09:23,120
The assembly line created for consumers

111
00:09:23,121 --> 00:09:28,920
a kind of abundance that the world had never seen before.

112
00:09:28,921 --> 00:09:33,860
Next up, Henry Ford picks up the pace.

113
00:09:33,861 --> 00:09:41,460
By 1926, Henry Ford operated assembly plants in 19 countries.

114
00:09:41,461 --> 00:09:44,160
Assembly Lines will continue on Modern Marvels.

115
00:09:53,220 --> 00:09:56,760
The wondrous technology of the assembly line effectively

116
00:09:56,761 --> 00:09:59,460
was its undoing relative to labor.

117
00:10:03,860 --> 00:10:08,260
Henry Ford invested millions in machines that mechanized most

118
00:10:08,360 --> 00:10:11,100
of the jobs on the moving assembly line.

119
00:10:11,200 --> 00:10:14,200
And while the production technique revolutionized

120
00:10:14,300 --> 00:10:17,200
manufacturing, workers hated it.

121
00:10:17,300 --> 00:10:18,600
It's boring.

122
00:10:18,700 --> 00:10:19,800
It's regimented.

123
00:10:19,900 --> 00:10:21,020
It's monotonous.

124
00:10:21,120 --> 00:10:23,320
It was very difficult to keep a lot of workers

125
00:10:23,420 --> 00:10:25,620
in these factories when they first started the moving

126
00:10:25,720 --> 00:10:27,520
assembly line.

127
00:10:27,620 --> 00:10:30,420
In the need for speed, highly skilled workers

128
00:10:30,421 --> 00:10:34,420
skilled workers were replaced by lower paid, unskilled workers,

129
00:10:34,421 --> 00:10:40,220
asked to perform simple tasks over and over and over again.

130
00:10:40,221 --> 00:10:46,020
Labor leader reported the comment of a Ford auto worker

131
00:10:46,021 --> 00:10:52,920
as saying, if I have to put on nut number 86 86 more times,

132
00:10:52,921 --> 00:10:56,820
I will be nut number 86 in the Pontiac Bug House.

133
00:10:56,821 --> 00:10:59,720
Work became degraded.

134
00:10:59,721 --> 00:11:02,380
Ford believed that mass production on an assembly line

135
00:11:02,381 --> 00:11:05,780
didn't require workers to think, so much as it required them

136
00:11:05,781 --> 00:11:07,220
to keep pace with the machines.

137
00:11:09,260 --> 00:11:14,480
A foreman in the Ford engine assembly plant

138
00:11:14,481 --> 00:11:20,620
talked about how in his shop, after they introduced line

139
00:11:20,621 --> 00:11:25,800
production, what he would do is he'd turn up the speed of the

140
00:11:25,801 --> 00:11:31,980
a notch, and the workers would move faster, turn it up another

141
00:11:31,981 --> 00:11:35,040
notch, turn it up another notch, and then the workers would

142
00:11:35,041 --> 00:11:37,220
scream, and they'd turn it back one notch.

143
00:11:37,221 --> 00:11:40,640
They said, we used to get 10, 15 more machines a day by doing

144
00:11:40,641 --> 00:11:43,020
that. Workers folded

145
00:11:43,021 --> 00:11:47,220
under the pressure. Turnover reached 380% by the end

146
00:11:47,221 --> 00:11:52,820
of 1913, and absenteeism was so frequent it became known as

147
00:11:52,821 --> 00:11:54,720
as Ford-itis.

148
00:11:54,721 --> 00:11:56,620
In essence, the unskilled workers

149
00:11:56,621 --> 00:11:59,400
had become as interchangeable on the assembly line

150
00:11:59,401 --> 00:12:02,360
as the parts themselves.

151
00:12:02,361 --> 00:12:05,760
Ford once said, every part followed a predetermined place

152
00:12:05,761 --> 00:12:07,340
through the plant.

153
00:12:07,341 --> 00:12:11,080
If you had a person missing from that particular place,

154
00:12:11,081 --> 00:12:14,000
the whole system stopped.

155
00:12:14,001 --> 00:12:18,020
The Ford plant had 14,000 workers.

156
00:12:18,021 --> 00:12:22,480
With a 10% absentee rate, you have to have 1,400

157
00:12:22,481 --> 00:12:26,080
workers to fill the assembly lines.

158
00:12:26,180 --> 00:12:29,880
People didn't want to do these jobs, and it required the bait

159
00:12:29,980 --> 00:12:33,420
of considerable increase in wages to keep people in those

160
00:12:33,520 --> 00:12:34,620
factories.

161
00:12:34,720 --> 00:12:36,920
Ford's response to the growing labor crisis

162
00:12:37,020 --> 00:12:38,420
made him a hero.

163
00:12:38,520 --> 00:12:42,340
He doubled assembly line wages to $5 a day and shortened the

164
00:12:42,440 --> 00:12:45,040
work day from 9 hours to 8.

165
00:12:45,140 --> 00:12:48,740
By 1914, the assembly line worker made five times more

166
00:12:48,840 --> 00:12:51,580
than the average American, whose annual income was an

167
00:12:51,581 --> 00:12:52,975
an estimated $334.

168
00:12:52,976 --> 00:12:58,940
The assembly line worker could now afford to buy the Model T he was building.

169
00:12:59,820 --> 00:13:01,010
It worked.

170
00:13:01,011 --> 00:13:09,820
Turnover dropped, absenteeism dropped, and Ford workers, you know, sort of

171
00:13:10,700 --> 00:13:13,554
traded the high wages for the rotten work.

172
00:13:13,555 --> 00:13:17,020
With the mechanical assembly line setting the pace,

173
00:13:17,021 --> 00:13:20,420
Ford enjoyed unparalleled success throughout the first two

174
00:13:20,421 --> 00:13:22,420
decades of the 20th century.

175
00:13:22,421 --> 00:13:26,420
In 1919, the assembly line helped make him the first

176
00:13:26,421 --> 00:13:27,420
billionaire.

177
00:13:27,421 --> 00:13:31,860
The assembly line has brought wealth and bounty, not just to

178
00:13:31,861 --> 00:13:37,380
Ford and Ford's workers, but also to Americans in general.

179
00:13:37,381 --> 00:13:43,380
There is perhaps no greater symbol of wealth and bounty

180
00:13:43,381 --> 00:13:48,380
than the monumental River Rouge complex in Dearborn, Michigan.

181
00:13:48,480 --> 00:13:52,380
Opened in 1918, many considered the plant to be Henry Ford's

182
00:13:52,480 --> 00:13:57,120
industrial masterpiece, the embodiment of mass production,

183
00:13:57,220 --> 00:14:00,140
and the assembly line technique on an epic scale.

184
00:14:03,140 --> 00:14:09,140
75,000 workers tended 53,000 machines along more than 30 miles

185
00:14:09,240 --> 00:14:12,780
of conveyor belts, making it the largest assembly line

186
00:14:12,781 --> 00:14:14,980
line in the world.

187
00:14:14,981 --> 00:14:17,960
They manufactured their own glass, steel, and coke

188
00:14:17,961 --> 00:14:19,320
for smelting.

189
00:14:19,321 --> 00:14:23,220
By 1928, a complete automobile from raw materials

190
00:14:23,221 --> 00:14:27,200
to finished product took only four days.

191
00:14:27,201 --> 00:14:29,600
I think what happens through the 1920s

192
00:14:29,601 --> 00:14:34,760
is that the line and the line speed and the regime

193
00:14:34,761 --> 00:14:41,720
of work in the factory becomes harder, faster, more ruthless.

194
00:14:41,721 --> 00:14:44,860
But the Rouge Factory was a magnet for mechanically minded

195
00:14:44,861 --> 00:14:48,300
young men fueled by fresh opportunity.

196
00:14:48,301 --> 00:14:51,420
Roosevelt Ford hopped a freight train north from Mississippi

197
00:14:51,421 --> 00:14:54,780
after he heard that the auto companies were hiring.

198
00:14:54,781 --> 00:14:58,600
In 1921, Roosevelt went to work for Ford.

199
00:14:58,601 --> 00:15:01,660
As a skilled laborer, he helped set up, maintain, and repair

200
00:15:01,661 --> 00:15:04,340
assembly lines at the Rouge complex.

201
00:15:04,341 --> 00:15:07,420
Back in the days when he was a millwright,

202
00:15:07,421 --> 00:15:11,580
Ford Motor Company was the only company in the big three

203
00:15:11,581 --> 00:15:15,960
that had any blacks in skilled trades.

204
00:15:15,961 --> 00:15:18,180
As a skilled laborer, Roosevelt Ford

205
00:15:18,181 --> 00:15:20,860
served as an inspiration to his family,

206
00:15:20,861 --> 00:15:23,640
so much so that four generations went on to work

207
00:15:23,641 --> 00:15:24,520
at the Rouge Complex.

208
00:15:28,740 --> 00:15:31,300
Seen here at a recent reunion, the family

209
00:15:31,301 --> 00:15:36,200
has a total of 300 years of automotive assembly experience.

210
00:15:36,201 --> 00:15:39,180
Strong and proud, they've not only survived,

211
00:15:39,181 --> 00:15:41,360
but thrived in the harsh reality of life

212
00:15:41,361 --> 00:15:43,200
on the assembly line.

213
00:15:43,201 --> 00:15:45,020
There's a lot of pressure on you.

214
00:15:45,021 --> 00:15:46,260
I feel a lot of pressure.

215
00:15:46,261 --> 00:15:49,600
Repetitive motion and strenuous standing up all day,

216
00:15:49,601 --> 00:15:50,900
I believe that's the hardest part,

217
00:15:50,901 --> 00:15:52,300
because it wears your body out.

218
00:15:52,301 --> 00:15:54,760
The line is going to run in space.

219
00:15:54,761 --> 00:15:56,160
You have to catch on.

220
00:15:56,161 --> 00:15:58,600
I don't think you really get the feel of this,

221
00:15:58,601 --> 00:16:00,940
even though you can look at people doing it,

222
00:16:00,941 --> 00:16:03,520
unless you've done it yourself.

223
00:16:03,521 --> 00:16:04,820
You really don't understand.

224
00:16:09,020 --> 00:16:11,360
All of the Fords agree that while today's assembly

225
00:16:11,361 --> 00:16:14,460
line is different from those in Roosevelt's day, he still helped

226
00:16:14,461 --> 00:16:18,760
to prepare them for the job. He used to tell us some of the

227
00:16:18,761 --> 00:16:22,940
stories about some of the things that went on before the union

228
00:16:22,941 --> 00:16:26,600
got into the shop. If the production line was stopped

229
00:16:26,601 --> 00:16:31,180
for any reason, it was like an investigation and it was nothing

230
00:16:31,181 --> 00:16:35,620
for a person to be fired to lose their job if they were found to

231
00:16:35,621 --> 00:16:37,960
be at fault. Prior to the union, if a person

232
00:16:37,961 --> 00:16:41,660
was seen spitting on the floor or smoking a cigarette,

233
00:16:41,661 --> 00:16:42,720
they could lose their job.

234
00:16:42,721 --> 00:16:45,160
You had to take a little bit of everything.

235
00:16:45,161 --> 00:16:47,220
You got out there and did it.

236
00:16:47,221 --> 00:16:48,400
You could have been tired.

237
00:16:48,401 --> 00:16:49,960
You could be sick.

238
00:16:49,961 --> 00:16:51,660
But you had to produce.

239
00:16:51,661 --> 00:16:55,960
I came across an account of a woman who said that, you know,

240
00:16:55,961 --> 00:17:00,600
my husband is not even allowed to leave to go to the toilet.

241
00:17:00,601 --> 00:17:06,820
They have a trough in the work area where he goes to urinate.

242
00:17:06,821 --> 00:17:10,820
By 1929, cars were coming off Ford assembly lines at the rate

243
00:17:10,821 --> 00:17:15,660
of one every 10 seconds, a pace so prolific it would ultimately

244
00:17:15,661 --> 00:17:17,500
lead labor to organize against it.

245
00:17:20,360 --> 00:17:22,640
One of the early demands of the union

246
00:17:22,641 --> 00:17:26,060
is that the box which controlled the speed of the line

247
00:17:26,061 --> 00:17:32,720
would have two keys, and that both workers and management

248
00:17:32,721 --> 00:17:34,280
would control one of the keys.

249
00:17:34,281 --> 00:17:37,880
So I mean, in some sense, what unionization is about

250
00:17:37,881 --> 00:17:41,040
is to try to stop and slow down that speed up.

251
00:17:41,041 --> 00:17:42,680
While control of line speed would

252
00:17:42,681 --> 00:17:45,520
become a cornerstone in the contentious and continuing

253
00:17:45,521 --> 00:17:48,120
battle between labor and management,

254
00:17:48,121 --> 00:17:51,180
one thing about the assembly line was certain.

255
00:17:51,181 --> 00:17:52,660
First of all, it was a job.

256
00:17:52,661 --> 00:17:53,920
I had a job.

257
00:17:53,921 --> 00:18:00,900
The assembly line did a lot for a lot of people.

258
00:18:00,901 --> 00:18:02,740
It was a phenomenal thing.

259
00:18:02,741 --> 00:18:06,100
I guess is the best way for me to put it.

260
00:18:06,101 --> 00:18:09,940
15 million Model Ts were built on Ford assembly lines,

261
00:18:09,941 --> 00:18:14,740
a record that lasted for nearly 50 years.

262
00:18:14,741 --> 00:18:17,140
Assembly lines will return on Modern Marvels.

263
00:18:17,141 --> 00:18:31,141
The assembly line was a tremendous symbol of modernity,

264
00:18:33,020 --> 00:18:36,100
of productivity, of wealth.

265
00:18:40,060 --> 00:18:43,560
By the late 20s, 43% of the world's goods

266
00:18:43,561 --> 00:18:45,440
were produced in America.

267
00:18:45,441 --> 00:18:48,900
as manufacturers of all kinds tried to emulate Henry Ford's

268
00:18:48,901 --> 00:18:52,140
assembly line technology. If you can make something as

269
00:18:52,141 --> 00:18:55,880
complicated as an automobile on an assembly line, the reasoning

270
00:18:55,881 --> 00:18:59,200
goes that I ought to be able to make toasters and phonographs

271
00:18:59,201 --> 00:19:01,940
and other things which should be much simpler.

272
00:19:02,940 --> 00:19:06,040
They look good, don't they? They taste good, too.

273
00:19:07,100 --> 00:19:10,060
Ford's method of mass production, however, required mass

274
00:19:10,061 --> 00:19:13,660
consumption in order to maximize profit and minimize cost.

275
00:19:13,661 --> 00:19:21,060
That simple formula would ultimately prove to be the undoing of some early manufacturers.

276
00:19:21,061 --> 00:19:24,660
But that didn't stop any of them from applying the new assembly line technology.

277
00:19:24,661 --> 00:19:32,420
There are new products coming on into the home, electrical appliances, which had been

278
00:19:32,421 --> 00:19:38,620
sort of experimented with at the turn of the century, but these were going in wholesale

279
00:19:38,621 --> 00:19:40,380
into American homes.

280
00:19:40,381 --> 00:19:46,600
You have companies like General Electric, Westinghouse that are beginning to make home

281
00:19:46,601 --> 00:19:49,620
appliances with moving line assembly.

282
00:19:49,621 --> 00:19:57,340
The assembly line allowed not only more stuff to be made, but more complicated stuff, higher

283
00:19:57,341 --> 00:20:05,660
quality stuff, things like radios and phonographs, things that had once been fairly high-end,

284
00:20:05,661 --> 00:20:10,360
abundance, a kind of material abundance that simply people had

285
00:20:10,361 --> 00:20:15,400
never experienced before. By 1927, the American factory

286
00:20:15,401 --> 00:20:18,400
worker had a third more purchasing power than a decade

287
00:20:18,401 --> 00:20:22,220
before. Newly created installment plans allowed

288
00:20:22,221 --> 00:20:27,120
consumers to snap up refrigerators, washing machines,

289
00:20:27,121 --> 00:20:30,220
automobiles and other goods as fast as they were coming off the

290
00:20:30,221 --> 00:20:37,500
assembly lines, with one exception. Foster Gunnison liked to see himself as

291
00:20:37,501 --> 00:20:44,860
the Henry Ford of housing. He built a factory that built houses, very simple

292
00:20:44,861 --> 00:20:53,020
houses with simple wall structures on a moving line basis. A lot of things that

293
00:20:53,021 --> 00:20:57,420
actually went into custom-built homes had actually been mass-produced, things like

294
00:20:57,421 --> 00:21:03,200
fixtures, things like bathtubs, etc., that those were already mass produced.

295
00:21:03,201 --> 00:21:08,180
A study conducted at that time concluded that for some reason, with regard to housing,

296
00:21:08,181 --> 00:21:12,960
Americans, quote, refused to allow their tastes to succumb to mass production techniques

297
00:21:12,961 --> 00:21:16,600
and its concomitant standardization, unquote.

298
00:21:16,601 --> 00:21:22,520
Consumers for the most part have rejected mass produced houses.

299
00:21:22,521 --> 00:21:26,480
In other words, Foster Gunnison was out of the assembly line housing business.

300
00:21:26,481 --> 00:21:30,080
While American consumers rejected homes built on the

301
00:21:30,081 --> 00:21:32,880
assembly line, they wholeheartedly embraced

302
00:21:32,881 --> 00:21:36,080
automobiles mass-produced in this way.

303
00:21:36,081 --> 00:21:39,080
We talk about car companies so much in this assembly line

304
00:21:39,081 --> 00:21:42,520
context because really that's where the assembly line was

305
00:21:42,521 --> 00:21:48,080
perfected and grew up, and it's where it matured.

306
00:21:48,081 --> 00:21:51,520
Ford's original assembly line was designed not so much to

307
00:21:51,521 --> 00:21:54,880
build cars as to build the Model T.

308
00:21:54,881 --> 00:21:58,680
But the Model T offered precious little choice to consumers and

309
00:21:58,681 --> 00:22:01,420
only came in one color, black.

310
00:22:01,421 --> 00:22:05,420
Sure, it was cheap, but now consumers wanted variety, and

311
00:22:05,421 --> 00:22:07,820
General Motors geared up to give it to them.

312
00:22:07,821 --> 00:22:10,920
One of the things that General Motors realized was nobody was

313
00:22:10,921 --> 00:22:14,140
going to compete with Henry Ford directly on price.

314
00:22:14,141 --> 00:22:18,140
So the key was to make a car that cost a little more than a

315
00:22:18,141 --> 00:22:21,340
Ford, but offered more than a Ford.

316
00:22:21,341 --> 00:22:24,540
And that's really where they tried to target the Chevrolet.

317
00:22:24,541 --> 00:22:28,140
What you see in the assembly line, in terms of engineering

318
00:22:28,141 --> 00:22:32,140
the assembly line, is essentially working out methods by which

319
00:22:32,141 --> 00:22:38,580
you can have greater variety. By 1926, Ford had lost 25%

320
00:22:38,581 --> 00:22:41,900
of the market share to General Motors, which was now offering

321
00:22:41,901 --> 00:22:45,500
the more expensive Chevrolet in a number of colors with a

322
00:22:45,501 --> 00:22:49,940
variety of options. In 1927, Ford reluctantly abandoned

323
00:22:49,941 --> 00:22:54,380
abandoned the reliable Model T and introduced a new model.

324
00:22:54,480 --> 00:22:58,980
It was an enormous, wrenching change to shut down his

325
00:22:59,080 --> 00:23:02,320
factories, retool them to produce the next car, which

326
00:23:02,420 --> 00:23:06,020
he called the Model A, and was a very different, much more

327
00:23:06,120 --> 00:23:08,120
modern automobile than the Model T.

328
00:23:08,220 --> 00:23:10,960
It was a colossal change for them.

329
00:23:11,060 --> 00:23:14,460
Not only was it expensive, but there was also a tremendous

330
00:23:14,560 --> 00:23:15,960
personnel cost.

331
00:23:16,060 --> 00:23:18,300
And they threw a lot of people out.

332
00:23:18,301 --> 00:23:20,460
They fired a lot of people, brought in different people.

333
00:23:21,400 --> 00:23:24,560
When they begin to assemble the Model A,

334
00:23:24,900 --> 00:23:28,840
they have tremendous problems making the assembly line work.

335
00:23:29,140 --> 00:23:33,160
The switchover to the Model A cost Ford more than a quarter of a billion dollars.

336
00:23:33,820 --> 00:23:36,180
Having produced the Model T for 19 years,

337
00:23:36,181 --> 00:23:41,260
the Model A required massive retooling for its 5,580 parts,

338
00:23:41,800 --> 00:23:43,100
almost all of which were new.

339
00:23:43,620 --> 00:23:47,060
It was a painfully unprofitable lesson in tracking consumer demand.

340
00:23:47,061 --> 00:23:58,500
General Motors was able to make those kinds of changes much more easily because they had never pursued things as far down that cul-de-sac as Ford Motor Company had.

341
00:24:00,020 --> 00:24:09,331
Unlike Ford with its monumental manufacturing plants, General Motors was able to make a variety of cars because they had several smaller facilities, with assembly

342
00:24:09,332 --> 00:24:11,400
lines dedicated to different models.

343
00:24:11,401 --> 00:24:15,400
The annual model change, which was introduced by General Motors,

344
00:24:15,401 --> 00:24:18,400
soon became a rite of passage for American motorists, and

345
00:24:18,401 --> 00:24:21,400
consumers had their wish for variety and style.

346
00:24:21,401 --> 00:24:24,400
After more than a decade, manufacturers had learned how to

347
00:24:24,401 --> 00:24:28,400
dedicate their assembly lines to changing consumer demands.

348
00:24:31,400 --> 00:24:35,400
And by 1941, during World War II, there was no bigger, more

349
00:24:35,401 --> 00:24:38,400
demanding consumer than the United States government.

350
00:24:38,401 --> 00:24:45,800
I think Lewis Mumford said that really there's no kind of demand like demand that comes about through warfare.

351
00:24:46,580 --> 00:24:51,320
Because, first of all, you need a lot of things and you destroy a lot of things.

352
00:24:51,580 --> 00:24:53,020
It creates this tremendous demand.

353
00:24:54,480 --> 00:24:57,220
Here is the arsenal of democracy at work.

354
00:24:57,820 --> 00:25:01,260
One plant among thousands dotted across the vast United States.

355
00:25:01,640 --> 00:25:04,820
Detroit's automotive assembly lines transform themselves.

356
00:25:05,720 --> 00:25:08,160
Effectively what you're doing is creating something entirely different.

357
00:25:08,400 --> 00:25:12,060
I mean, that process of conversion over to wartime industry

358
00:25:12,061 --> 00:25:19,500
is one where the whole shop floor is just literally in turmoil.

359
00:25:19,501 --> 00:25:25,200
An industry which had been geared to produce the 32 million cars currently in use by civilians,

360
00:25:25,201 --> 00:25:33,000
refocused, retooled, and renewed its commitment to unleash the inexhaustible power of the wartime assembly line.

361
00:25:33,001 --> 00:25:36,800
Plants that build tanks like these, huge lumbering juggernauts,

362
00:25:36,801 --> 00:25:39,800
in numbers that stagger the imagination.

363
00:25:41,800 --> 00:25:43,800
If you look at the history of the war,

364
00:25:43,801 --> 00:25:48,800
you'll see that the United States created tremendous amount of war material.

365
00:25:48,801 --> 00:25:52,800
World War II also sees the coming in of large numbers of women

366
00:25:52,801 --> 00:25:55,800
and large numbers of African Americans.

367
00:25:55,801 --> 00:26:01,800
Prior to that time, it was believed that neither women nor African Americans

368
00:26:01,801 --> 00:26:05,800
had the skills to work the machinery and the heavy equipment

369
00:26:05,801 --> 00:26:07,060
that was tied to the industry.

370
00:26:10,980 --> 00:26:13,940
There's a lot of contention in the auto industry

371
00:26:13,941 --> 00:26:15,660
through the war years.

372
00:26:16,320 --> 00:26:18,880
As African-American workers are upgraded

373
00:26:18,881 --> 00:26:21,660
to the machines and the assembly lines,

374
00:26:21,800 --> 00:26:24,300
there are sometimes what are referred to as hate strikes.

375
00:26:25,000 --> 00:26:27,860
White workers who would go on strike

376
00:26:27,861 --> 00:26:29,800
because they didn't want to see the upgrading

377
00:26:29,801 --> 00:26:31,700
of African-American workers.

378
00:26:31,780 --> 00:26:33,780
And the same thing occurred with women workers sometimes.

379
00:26:33,781 --> 00:26:38,980
In spite of an openly hostile environment, the capabilities of

380
00:26:38,981 --> 00:26:42,320
blacks and women were clearly demonstrated by their output.

381
00:26:43,460 --> 00:26:47,640
This is mass production as only American engineers know how.

382
00:26:48,600 --> 00:26:53,340
By the end of the war in 1945, they had produced 90,000 bombers,

383
00:26:53,940 --> 00:26:58,060
57,000 aircraft engines, a quarter million tanks and jeeps.

384
00:26:59,200 --> 00:27:02,360
One of the major reasons that we won World War II

385
00:27:02,361 --> 00:27:07,840
was because we were able to produce all kinds of war material,

386
00:27:08,300 --> 00:27:12,780
from tanks and airplanes to clothing and helmets,

387
00:27:13,980 --> 00:27:16,760
at a faster rate, at a cheaper price,

388
00:27:16,900 --> 00:27:18,720
than any other nation on Earth.

389
00:27:19,620 --> 00:27:23,640
So we were able to build ships faster than the Japanese

390
00:27:23,641 --> 00:27:25,880
and the Germans combined could sink them.

391
00:27:28,180 --> 00:27:32,200
Next, post-war Japan reconstructs the assembly line.

392
00:27:32,360 --> 00:27:39,440
During World War II, women and black assembly line workers earned only 60% of the white

393
00:27:39,441 --> 00:27:41,560
male wage.

394
00:27:41,561 --> 00:27:52,880
Assembly Lines will return on Modern Marvels.

395
00:27:52,881 --> 00:27:57,740
I think you could say that Henry Ford invented the assembly line for the car and that Toyota

396
00:27:57,741 --> 00:27:58,740
perfected it.

397
00:27:58,741 --> 00:28:05,740
After World War II, U.S. productivity was said to be eight

398
00:28:05,741 --> 00:28:08,740
times greater than that of Japan, which was ravaged by

399
00:28:08,741 --> 00:28:10,740
depression and inflation.

400
00:28:10,741 --> 00:28:13,740
It took a situation in the postwar period back in the

401
00:28:13,741 --> 00:28:16,740
early 50s. They really had nothing in terms

402
00:28:16,741 --> 00:28:19,740
of resources. It was a resource-poor country.

403
00:28:19,741 --> 00:28:23,740
They had no opportunity to waste. Any materials they had,

404
00:28:23,741 --> 00:28:25,740
they had to somehow use.

405
00:28:25,741 --> 00:28:28,440
In 1950, E.G.

406
00:28:28,441 --> 00:28:32,080
Toyota, then a young Japanese engineer hoping to rebuild his

407
00:28:32,081 --> 00:28:36,340
family's business, spent three months at the Rouge Complex, the

408
00:28:36,341 --> 00:28:40,480
most efficient car manufacturing facility in the world.

409
00:28:40,481 --> 00:28:43,900
Unlike many manufacturers at that time, Henry Ford was

410
00:28:43,901 --> 00:28:46,500
unafraid to share the unprecedented scale of his

411
00:28:46,501 --> 00:28:50,840
assembly line techniques with other industrialists.

412
00:28:50,841 --> 00:28:53,940
Ford's assembly line worked great when you had high volume

413
00:28:53,941 --> 00:28:56,140
in low variety, hopefully no variety, just one.

414
00:28:56,480 --> 00:28:57,780
But Toyota's situation was different.

415
00:28:58,800 --> 00:29:00,860
At that time, Toyota Motor Company

416
00:29:00,861 --> 00:29:02,860
was making 1,000 cars a month,

417
00:29:03,200 --> 00:29:05,680
while the Rouge was making 1,000 cars a day.

418
00:29:06,160 --> 00:29:07,860
And that wasn't Toyota's only problem.

419
00:29:08,580 --> 00:29:10,520
Here they are making 1,000 cars per month.

420
00:29:11,260 --> 00:29:13,000
And all those, they had to make all kinds of cars.

421
00:29:13,140 --> 00:29:14,880
They had to make trucks, big trucks, little trucks,

422
00:29:15,220 --> 00:29:17,800
big cars, little cars, limousines, fire engines,

423
00:29:17,920 --> 00:29:19,060
you name it, they had to make everything

424
00:29:19,061 --> 00:29:20,100
for the Japanese market.

425
00:29:20,300 --> 00:29:23,420
It's necessity that compels the Japanese

426
00:29:23,421 --> 00:29:27,880
to redefine the responsibilities of labor in a system that is starved for capital

427
00:29:27,881 --> 00:29:30,840
and is only serving very low volumes of demand.

428
00:29:31,400 --> 00:29:34,700
Unlike the highly mechanized assembly lines developed in Detroit,

429
00:29:35,440 --> 00:29:39,320
Toyota built their assembly lines around their primary resource, which was labor.

430
00:29:39,840 --> 00:29:44,240
And to do that, they realized the most flexible thing in the entire operation are people.

431
00:29:44,900 --> 00:29:46,720
Not machines, not anything else, it's people.

432
00:29:46,721 --> 00:29:52,220
The Japanese never went as far down this road towards making the worker

433
00:29:52,221 --> 00:29:55,180
merely a cog in the machine as we did.

434
00:29:55,181 --> 00:29:58,120
They were actually using their workers' mind power,

435
00:29:58,121 --> 00:30:00,620
and we had forgotten about that.

436
00:30:00,621 --> 00:30:02,660
In what was considered a fundamental change

437
00:30:02,661 --> 00:30:05,960
in the assembly line technique, Toyota reorganized the work,

438
00:30:05,961 --> 00:30:08,560
reduced the waste, and let the workers define their

439
00:30:08,561 --> 00:30:12,280
responsibilities in a process called lean manufacturing.

440
00:30:12,281 --> 00:30:14,680
Every job that you see on a Toyota assembly line

441
00:30:14,681 --> 00:30:17,580
has been slowly and steadily improved and fine-tuned over

442
00:30:17,581 --> 00:30:20,180
the years by every operator who's done those jobs.

443
00:30:20,181 --> 00:30:23,220
Toyota managed to involve the workers, to engage the workers,

444
00:30:23,221 --> 00:30:27,180
and to give them jobs that actually provide more fulfillment on the assembly line.

445
00:30:28,180 --> 00:30:31,680
Not only were workers required to define and improve their jobs,

446
00:30:31,681 --> 00:30:34,180
they were given a variety of jobs to perform.

447
00:30:35,680 --> 00:30:39,180
They do low-end maintenance, they do low-end housekeeping.

448
00:30:39,181 --> 00:30:42,680
That means that you have much less support staff in the factory.

449
00:30:42,681 --> 00:30:47,180
You have fewer material handlers, you have fewer janitors,

450
00:30:47,181 --> 00:30:48,980
You have fewer maintenance personnel.

451
00:30:48,981 --> 00:30:50,480
You have fewer inspectors.

452
00:30:50,481 --> 00:30:52,780
Tura said the operators, the plant floor,

453
00:30:52,781 --> 00:30:53,980
is in charge of quality.

454
00:30:53,981 --> 00:30:56,480
If you have a problem, you stop the line right now.

455
00:30:58,860 --> 00:31:01,200
With that, every worker on the assembly line

456
00:31:01,201 --> 00:31:02,960
became an inspector.

457
00:31:02,961 --> 00:31:04,600
Quality and productivity increased

458
00:31:04,601 --> 00:31:06,800
because assemblers were able to make more parts

459
00:31:06,801 --> 00:31:08,540
right the first time.

460
00:31:08,541 --> 00:31:10,940
The productivity tends to be higher

461
00:31:10,941 --> 00:31:14,240
in a lean production plant because you are obligating

462
00:31:14,241 --> 00:31:16,780
workers to take on tasks which previously

463
00:31:16,781 --> 00:31:20,620
have been carried out by indirect support staff.

464
00:31:20,621 --> 00:31:23,180
Toyota also did away with certain specialists on the

465
00:31:23,181 --> 00:31:27,020
assembly line, like die changers. A die is a hard piece of

466
00:31:27,021 --> 00:31:30,620
metal in the exact shape sheet metal assumes after pounding.

467
00:31:30,621 --> 00:31:34,140
Toyota developed a simple die change technique on rollers that

468
00:31:34,141 --> 00:31:37,820
allowed workers to change dies in two to three hours instead of

469
00:31:37,821 --> 00:31:41,340
the two or three weeks it took at American Auto plants.

470
00:31:41,341 --> 00:31:44,220
They didn't know exactly what number of different products

471
00:31:44,221 --> 00:31:45,820
they were going to be making every day.

472
00:31:45,821 --> 00:31:48,680
So they had to be prepared to make those every single day.

473
00:31:49,100 --> 00:31:54,420
The Toyota assembly line required that its workers be skilled, motivated, and innovative.

474
00:31:55,060 --> 00:31:59,960
Because once production was established, the workers were responsible for continued improvement,

475
00:32:00,600 --> 00:32:02,380
a process they call Kaizen.

476
00:32:02,900 --> 00:32:09,320
You would remove 10% of the workers, 10% of the time, 10% of the resources that backed up that process,

477
00:32:09,321 --> 00:32:13,520
and force the remaining workers to figure out how to now get that job done

478
00:32:13,521 --> 00:32:15,680
with 90% of what they had before.

479
00:32:15,860 --> 00:32:17,160
And so that would be Kaizen.

480
00:32:17,420 --> 00:32:19,620
That would be the continuous improvement element of it.

481
00:32:19,920 --> 00:32:22,160
Once they had reestablished a certain balance,

482
00:32:22,280 --> 00:32:23,960
then you take out 10% again.

483
00:32:24,560 --> 00:32:28,540
And you continually ratchet downward the resources available.

484
00:32:28,700 --> 00:32:30,320
That is what makes it lean,

485
00:32:30,321 --> 00:32:34,580
because you have only the bare minimum necessary to get the job done.

486
00:32:34,660 --> 00:32:37,800
And you keep pressing it downward to see what you can achieve

487
00:32:37,801 --> 00:32:42,160
in the way of maximum efficiency in terms of productivity.

488
00:32:43,520 --> 00:32:47,520
